course: world
lesson: 191
title: A fair trial, and the Wonderland court that gets it all wrong
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: At the end of «Alice's Adventures in Wonderland» the Knave of Hearts is put on trial for stealing the Queen's tarts. Alice has read about courts in books and proudly knows the judge by his wig and the twelve jurors in the jury-box, and then she watches the court break almost every rule of a fair trial.
explain: In a fair trial, the person accused is treated as innocent until it is proved, with evidence, that they did it. Witnesses tell what they saw, the jury listens to all of it, and only then does the jury give its verdict: guilty or not guilty. Only after a guilty verdict does the judge give the sentence, the punishment.
explain: The Queen shouts «Sentence first, verdict afterwards!», the judge is her own husband, the King, the witnesses are threatened, the King invents a rule on the spot, and an unsigned poem that names nobody is called proof. Alice shouts «Stuff and nonsense!», and she is right.
explain: Laughing at this court is a good way to learn why the real rules exist: each one stops an unfair punishment, which could happen to anyone, even you, when you are blamed for something you did not do at school.
fact: A trial is a meeting in a court where it is decided, by careful rules, whether someone did break the law.
fact: In England and Wales a jury in a serious criminal trial is twelve ordinary adults; in Scotland it is fifteen.
fact: «Innocent until proven guilty» means the person accused does not have to prove they are innocent: the accusers must prove they are guilty.
fact: Evidence is anything that helps show what really happened: things that were found, what witnesses saw, written papers, pictures or recordings.
fact: A witness is someone who tells the court what they saw or know, and they must tell the truth; threatening a witness is a crime.
fact: The verdict is the jury's decision, guilty or not guilty; the sentence is the punishment the judge gives, and it can only come after a guilty verdict.
fact: A judge must be fair and must not judge a case in which he or his family is involved; in the book the judge is the King, and the tarts belong to his own wife, the Queen.
fact: In a fair court the rules are written down before the trial and are the same for everyone; nobody may invent a rule in the middle to win.
fact: In the book the King suddenly reads out «Rule Forty-two: all persons more than a mile high to leave the court», and when he says it is the oldest rule in the book, Alice says «Then it ought to be Number One».
fact: The King says that if the Knave did not sign the poem, that «only makes the matter worse»; but an unsigned paper that names nobody proves nothing about who wrote it.
fact: British judges have worn wigs and robes for hundreds of years as a tradition; in the book the King wears his crown on top of his wig and looks very uncomfortable.
fact: The jurors in the book write their names on slates «for fear they should forget them», which is Carroll making fun of juries that do not pay attention.
fact: In the book nobody is really executed: the Gryphon says the Queen's orders are «all her fancy», and the King quietly pardons everyone.
exercise: What is a jury? | Ordinary adults who decide guilty or not guilty | The people who arrested the accused person | A group of judges from other countries | The lawyers who argue each side
exercise: How many people sit on a jury in a serious English trial? | Twelve | Six | Three | Ten
exercise: What does «innocent until proven guilty» mean? | The accusers must prove guilt; the accused needn't prove innocence | The accused must prove they did not do it, or they are guilty | Everyone is innocent until the judge says they are not | Only children are innocent, and adults must prove it
exercise: Which of these is evidence? | A video of what happened | The judge's favourite colour | How the accused person dresses | Whether the jury like the accused
exercise: What must a witness do in court? | Tell the truth about what they saw or know | Say whatever helps their friends | Guess when they do not know the answer | Agree with whatever the judge says
exercise: What is a verdict? | The jury's decision: guilty or not guilty | The punishment the judge gives at the very end of a trial | The name of the person accused | The judge's wig and robes
exercise: What is a sentence in court? | The punishment, given after a guilty verdict | The first words the judge says when a trial starts each day | A list of the jurors' names | The story the accused tells about the day
exercise: What is wrong with «Sentence first, verdict afterwards»? | You can't punish someone before deciding if they're guilty | Verdicts are always given on the morning before trials begin, by law | Sentences are only given by the jury, not by queens | Nothing, that is how all real courts do it
exercise: Why should the King not be the judge in the tarts trial? | The tarts belonged to his wife, so he is not neutral | Kings are not allowed to wear wigs in court | He was too short to see over the bench | Judges must always be women in English law
exercise: What is wrong with the King's «Rule Forty-two»? | He made it up during the trial | It was too short to be a real rule | Rules must always have even numbers | It was written in French, not English
exercise: What does the unsigned poem prove about the Knave? | Nothing, as it names no one | That he wrote it, as he didn't sign | That he is a perfectly honest man | That he secretly loves the Queen
exercise: Why do judges in Britain wear wigs? | It is an old tradition | Judges are all bald underneath | Wigs help them hear better | It is a rule of science
exercise: What are the jurors in the book writing on their slates? | Their own names, so they won't forget them | The evidence, carefully, word by word | A letter asking to go home for tea | The answer to the Hatter's riddle
exercise: Who does Alice say is the judge, and how does she know? | The King, by his great wig | The Queen, by her crown | The Rabbit, by his trumpet | The Gryphon, by its wings
exercise: What does the Gryphon say about the Queen's orders to cut off heads? | They are all her fancy; nobody is executed | Every order is carried out the very same day | Only the gardeners are ever executed | The King carries them out himself at night
test: «The teacher said Jake broke the window, so he must have, end of» What would a fair trial say? | First look at the evidence and hear Jake; being accused is not being guilty | Teachers are always right, so Jake is guilty and should be punished | Jake must prove he was at home that day, or he is guilty | Ask the class to vote on whether Jake did it
test: A boy is blamed for stealing biscuits, and his mum says, «Right, no telly for a week, now tell me what happened». Which Wonderland mistake is she making? | Sentence first, verdict afterwards | Letting a juror forget his name | Calling an unsigned poem proof | Inventing a brand-new Rule Forty-two
test: «ref's mum is the captain of the other team, mate, that's well dodgy» Which rule of fair trials is this like? | A judge must not judge a case his own family is in | A judge must always wear a wig and a robe | A jury must always have twelve people in it | Witnesses must always be threatened first, to make sure they talk
test: In a playground game, the losing side suddenly says, «New rule: anyone with red trainers is out». Which part of the trial is it like? | The King inventing Rule Forty-two | The jury writing their names | The Queen's tarts going missing | The Hatter forgetting which day of the month it is
test: The King says the Knave's poem is unsigned, «so he must have meant some mischief». Why is that bad thinking? | Having no name on it proves nothing about who wrote it | Poems are never used as evidence in any court | The Knave could not write at all, because he was only a playing card | Unsigned poems are always written by the Queen
test: The King tells the Hatter, «Don't be nervous, or I'll have you executed on the spot». Why would that be wrong in a real court? | Threatening a witness is a crime, and scared witnesses may not tell the truth | Witnesses must never be told to stay calm | Hatters are not allowed to be witnesses in court | Only the jury may speak to witnesses
test: Which is the right order in a real trial? | Evidence, then verdict, then sentence if guilty | Sentence first, then the verdict, then all the evidence at the end | Verdict, then evidence, then sentence | Sentence, then evidence, then verdict
test: Mia says, «they wear wigs cos the law says the wig makes them fair». What is true? | Wigs are just an old tradition; fairness comes from the rules | The wig really does make a judge fairer | Judges without wigs are not allowed to give a verdict, so they must always wear them | Only the Queen can decide who wears a wig
test: Alice says Rule Forty-two «ought to be Number One» if it is the oldest. Why is that a clever answer? | Rules are numbered in order, so the oldest rule would be the first | Forty-two is a number that cannot be used for rules | Alice wanted to be the one who wrote and numbered all the rules of the court herself | Rule Number One is always about people's height
test: What would happen in a fair court if there was no real evidence against the Knave? | He would be found not guilty and go free | He would be found guilty anyway, to be safe | He would have to prove he never ate a tart | The judge would choose a punishment at random
test: ★★★ A juror writes «stupid» on her slate and then can't spell it, so asks her neighbour. In the book, what does this show about the jury? | They are not following the trial, so they cannot judge it fairly | They are the cleverest people in Wonderland | They have already been told the verdict by the Queen and are only pretending to listen | They are writing the sentence before the verdict
test: ★★★ The Queen's tarts are on the table in the court at the start of the trial, and the King points to them at the end. Why does that make the whole trial even sillier? | The tarts were never lost, so there may be no theft at all | The tarts prove the Knave ate them all | The tarts show that the Hatter baked them for his never-ending tea-party | The tarts were the jury's lunch
exam: Identify the principle denoted by the expression «innocent until proven guilty». | The burden of proving guilt rests upon the accuser and not the accused | The accused is obliged to demonstrate his own innocence | All persons present in court are presumed to be guilty until the conclusion of the proceedings | The judge determines guilt prior to hearing the evidence
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relation between verdict and sentence? | A sentence may be pronounced only after a verdict of guilty has been reached | A sentence must invariably be pronounced before the verdict is considered | A verdict and a sentence are identical terms for the same decision | A sentence is determined by the witnesses and the verdict by the judge alone, without a jury
exam: Select the response consistent with the established principles of fair adjudication. | A judge ought not to preside over a case in which his own family has an interest | A judge ought to preside over cases concerning his own household | A judge may introduce new rules during the proceedings whenever he considers it to be to his own advantage | A judge may threaten witnesses who appear nervous
exam: Ascertain the value, as evidence, of a document which bears no signature and names no person. | It does not in itself establish who composed it | It conclusively establishes the guilt of the accused | It establishes that the accused is an honest man | It establishes that the trial must be abandoned
